# Rank 1851 / CVE-2017-5581: TigerVNC's VNC viewer heap-overflows its
# framebuffer through an RRE framebuffer-update subrectangle that a hostile
# server sends outside the framebuffer bounds. The decoder
# (common/rfb/rreDecode.h, instantiated as rreDecode8/16/32 by
# common/rfb/RREDecoder.cxx) reads each subrectangle's position and size
# straight off the RFB stream:
#
#   int x = is->readU16();
#   int y = is->readU16();
#   int w = is->readU16();
#   int h = is->readU16();
#   pb->fillRect(pf, Rect(r.tl.x+x, r.tl.y+y, r.tl.x+x+w, r.tl.y+y+h), &pix);
#
# and ModifiablePixelBuffer::fillRect (common/rfb/PixelBuffer.cxx:99 at the
# parent) turns that Rect into a write extent with no clamp:
#
#   w = r.width();
#   h = r.height();
#   buf = getBufferRW(r, &stride);
#   ... memset(buf, *(const U8*)pix, w); ... memcpy(buf, start, w * b);
#
# so subrectangle coordinates past the framebuffer write past the allocation
# (CWE-787, heap). The fixing commit 18c020124f1b2441f714da2017f63dba50720ba
# changes only the callee: drect = r; if (!drect.enclosed_by(getRect()))
# drect = drect.intersect(getRect()); if (drect.is_empty()) return; -- the
# same clamp the sibling maskRect had, applied before getBufferRW.
# The pool row is correct: repository, language, CVE and commit all check,
# the vulnerable construct is present at 18c0201^ and the remediation at
# 18c0201. No corpus correction.
#
# What fires today. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB`
# over the whole tree reports 1083 findings; over three runs per revision the
# unique-fingerprint set is 1098 on the parent and 1098 at the fixed revision, and the
# only rows that differ are eleven displaced-by-eleven-lines presence rows in
# PixelBuffer.cxx itself (six VYQL-MEM-016 on the memcpy calls, which exist
# in both revisions, and five VYQL-MEM-006 NullableDeref rows, likewise on
# both sides). Nothing on rreDecode.h or RREDecoder.cxx differs at all: the
# pair is indistinguishable to the scanner, and the rows inside fillRect are
# "a memcpy exists", not the defect.
#
# Vocabulary is checked for reuse before anything is written. No binding
# anywhere labels rdr::InStream's readU16/readU32/readOpaque* (grep over all
# of vyql/bindings/), so the RFB stream is not a source. A probe binding
# (callee.method in [readU16, readU32, readOpaque16, readOpaque32] emitting
# code.HttpInput at call.result) is built and measured on the real tree
# anyway: it labels all five reader calls, and the full-tree fingerprint sets
# stay identical modulo the same eleven displaced rows -- zero findings
# change, because the taint path dies two hops downstream (below), so the
# binding is discarded. The sink half of the rule that would judge this
# shape, VYQL-MEM-003 (core.UserControlledData -> code.RawMemoryCopySizeSink),
# exists for memcpy/memmove/strncpy/strncat at args[2] but not memset, so the
# bpp==8 branch's write is unlabelled even where taint exists. And no C/C++
# binding emits core.MemoryBoundsCheck (only one exists, in Go), so even with
# the source labelled and every hop working, the clamp in the fixed form has
# no control that could clear the finding: intersect/enclosed_by results
# still derive from the attacker-supplied rect and the rule would fire on
# both revisions.
#
# Where the taint path actually stops, measured with minimal pairs.
#
#  1. A C++ member function defined out of line (`void C::m(...) { ... }` as
#     a top-level definition, the ordinary .h-declares/.cxx-defines layout
#     PixelBuffer.cxx itself uses) extracts nameless: declName
#     (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go:878) walks
#     pointer/array/parenthesized/init/function_declarator but has no
#     qualified_identifier case, and its default branch finds no `declarator`
#     field on one, so the name comes back "". The body is walked (its memcpy
#     keeps its syntactic sink label) but registers as an anonymous function
#     with no C.m#param nodes, so no call can link into it: `vyql trace` on
#     the reduced shape dead-ends at "Call ... call=pb.fillRect -- no
#     outgoing flow (callee not traced -- external or cross-package)".
#     Minimal pair below (member_inline vs member_outofline): identical
#     class, call and sink, definition moved in-class vs out of line -- the
#     inline form reports VYQL-MEM-003, the out-of-line form reports nothing,
#     stable over six runs. This is adjacent to but distinct from the
#     recorded constructor/operator-overload resolution gap: those are call
#     forms that do not resolve; here the callee body is never registered
#     under any name. Member-call linking itself is by bare method name
#     across classes (a g.copyN call links into a same-named other class's
#     inline method), which is what makes the inline half of the pair work
#     and also why two same-named methods in one file subsume each other.
#  2. Taint that enters a callee inside an aggregate passed by const
#     reference does not come back out through field reads or accessor
#     calls: the Rect constructor's arguments are tainted at the call site
#     (VYQL-NUM-003/017 fire on the adds), the aggregate reaches the
#     callee's parameter, and yet memcpy(..., r.width()),
#     memcpy(..., r.x2 - r.x) and w = r.width(); memcpy(..., w) all stay
#     clean -- only the identical function taking the extent as a plain int
#     parameter reports. Minimal pair below (extent_by_int vs
#     extent_by_rect). This is a field-sensitivity relation the engine does
#     not have for C/C++ aggregates, i.e. new dataflow, not a missing case.
#
# Cross-file free-function calls do resolve (an inline header function
# called from another file carries taint to its memcpy), so the
# RREDecoder.cxx -> rreDecode.h hop is not a blocker; the blockers are the
# member-definition extraction and the aggregate hop, and behind them the
# missing source binding and the missing clamp control. Four stacked gaps,
# one of them a new dataflow relation: not mechanical, so this rank is
# recorded ATTENTION rather than worked around.
#
# A callee-side presence binding ("getBufferRW user with no intersect") is
# considered and rejected on the library's own documentation: PixelBuffer.h
# documents these operations as "Basic rendering operations / These
# operations DO NOT clip to the pixelbuffer area, or trap overruns", and at
# the fixed revision's imageRect still calls getBufferRW(r, ...) with no clamp --
# a binding keying on the missing clamp would fire on the still-documented
# no-clip sibling at the fixed revision, and the caller that actually fails
# to validate (rreDecode.h) is byte-identical in both revisions, so a
# caller-side label cannot discriminate a fix that lands entirely inside the
# callee.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap. A future fix must flip
# rre_vuln, rre_vuln_bound_source, member_outofline and extent_by_rect to
# expect (the last two need only their named engine fix; the first two need
# the source binding and the clamp control as well), and must keep
# fillRect_fixed, member_inline and extent_by_int in their current states
# (member_inline and extent_by_int expect today and must keep reporting).

test "rank1851: RRE subrectangle geometry reaches fillRect with no framebuffer clamp"
  lang cpp
  file rre_vuln.cxx
  reject VYQL-MEM-003
  code
  ```
  #include <string.h>

  namespace rdr { class InStream { public: unsigned readU16(); unsigned readU32(); }; }

  class Rect {
  public:
    int x, y, x2, y2;
    int width() const { return x2 - x; }
    int height() const { return y2 - y; }
  };

  class ModifiablePixelBuffer {
  public:
    void fillRect(const Rect& r, const void* pix);
    unsigned char* getBufferRW(const Rect& r, int* stride);
    void commitBufferRW(const Rect& r);
  };

  // PixelBuffer.cxx:99 at the parent: the write extent comes straight from
  // the caller's Rect with no enclosed_by/intersect clamp.
  void ModifiablePixelBuffer::fillRect(const Rect& r, const void* pix)
  {
    int stride;
    unsigned char *buf;
    int w, h, b;

    w = r.width();
    h = r.height();
    b = 4;

    if (h == 0)
      return;

    buf = getBufferRW(r, &stride);

    if (b == 1) {
      while (h--) {
        memset(buf, *(const unsigned char*)pix, w);
        buf += stride * b;
      }
    } else {
      unsigned char *start;
      int w1;

      start = buf;

      w1 = w;
      while (w1--) {
        memcpy(buf, pix, b);
        buf += b;
      }
      buf += (stride - w) * b;
      h--;

      while (h--) {
        memcpy(buf, start, w * b);
        buf += stride * b;
      }
    }

    commitBufferRW(r);
  }

  // rreDecode.h: subrectangle position and size read off the RFB stream.
  void rreDecode(const Rect& r, rdr::InStream* is, ModifiablePixelBuffer* pb)
  {
    int nSubrects = is->readU32();
    for (int i = 0; i < nSubrects; i++) {
      int x = is->readU16();
      int y = is->readU16();
      int w = is->readU16();
      int h = is->readU16();
      pb->fillRect(Rect(r.x+x, r.y+y, r.x+x+w, r.y+y+h), 0);
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1851: same shape with a labelled source isolates the engine hops"
  lang cpp
  file rre_vuln_bound_source.cxx
  reject VYQL-MEM-003
  code
  ```
  #include <string.h>
  #include <sys/socket.h>

  class Rect {
  public:
    int x, y, x2, y2;
    int width() const { return x2 - x; }
    int height() const { return y2 - y; }
  };

  class ModifiablePixelBuffer {
  public:
    void fillRect(const Rect& r, const void* pix);
    unsigned char* getBufferRW(const Rect& r, int* stride);
    void commitBufferRW(const Rect& r);
  };

  void ModifiablePixelBuffer::fillRect(const Rect& r, const void* pix)
  {
    int stride;
    unsigned char *buf;
    int w, h, b;

    w = r.width();
    h = r.height();
    b = 4;

    buf = getBufferRW(r, &stride);

    while (h--) {
      memcpy(buf, buf, w * b);
      buf += stride * b;
    }

    commitBufferRW(r);
  }

  // The coordinates come from a bound source, so only the member-call hop
  // and the aggregate hop can block the path here.
  void rreDecodeBound(const Rect& r, int sock, ModifiablePixelBuffer* pb)
  {
    unsigned char wire[8];
    recv(sock, wire, 8, 0);
    Rect sub(r.x + wire[0], r.y + wire[1],
             r.x + wire[0] + wire[2], r.y + wire[1] + wire[3]);
    pb->fillRect(sub, 0);
  }
  ```

test "rank1851: the fixed fillRect clamps the rect before taking the buffer"
  lang cpp
  file fillRect_fixed.cxx
  reject VYQL-MEM-003
  code
  ```
  #include <string.h>

  namespace rdr { class InStream { public: unsigned readU16(); unsigned readU32(); }; }

  class Rect {
  public:
    int x, y, x2, y2;
    Rect() : x(0), y(0), x2(0), y2(0) {}
    Rect(int x_, int y_, int x2_, int y2_) : x(x_), y(y_), x2(x2_), y2(y2_) {}
    int width() const { return x2 - x; }
    int height() const { return y2 - y; }
    bool is_empty() const { return (x >= x2) || (y >= y2); }
    bool enclosed_by(const Rect& r) const { return x >= r.x && y >= r.y && x2 <= r.x2 && y2 <= r.y2; }
    Rect intersect(const Rect& r) const;
  };

  class ModifiablePixelBuffer {
  public:
    void fillRect(const Rect& r, const void* pix);
    unsigned char* getBufferRW(const Rect& r, int* stride);
    void commitBufferRW(const Rect& r);
    Rect getRect() const;
  };

  // PixelBuffer.cxx at the fix: the destination is intersected with the
  // framebuffer and an empty result returns before any write.
  void ModifiablePixelBuffer::fillRect(const Rect& r, const void* pix)
  {
    int stride;
    unsigned char *buf;
    int w, h, b;
    Rect drect;

    drect = r;
    if (!drect.enclosed_by(getRect())) {
      drect = drect.intersect(getRect());
    }

    if (drect.is_empty())
      return;

    w = drect.width();
    h = drect.height();
    b = 4;

    buf = getBufferRW(drect, &stride);

    while (h--) {
      memcpy(buf, buf, w * b);
      buf += stride * b;
    }

    commitBufferRW(drect);
  }

  void rreDecode(const Rect& r, rdr::InStream* is, ModifiablePixelBuffer* pb)
  {
    int nSubrects = is->readU32();
    for (int i = 0; i < nSubrects; i++) {
      int x = is->readU16();
      int y = is->readU16();
      int w = is->readU16();
      int h = is->readU16();
      pb->fillRect(Rect(r.x+x, r.y+y, r.x+x+w, r.y+y+h), 0);
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1851: minimal pair, member method defined in class -- the path works"
  lang cpp
  file member_inline.cxx
  expect VYQL-MEM-003
  code
  ```
  #include <string.h>
  #include <sys/socket.h>

  // The only difference from member_outofline.cxx is that copyN's body sits
  // inside the class declaration.
  class FbWriter {
  public:
    void copyN(int n, unsigned char* b) { memcpy(b, b, n); }
  };

  void drive(int sock)
  {
    unsigned char tmp[64];
    int n = recv(sock, tmp, 64, 0);
    FbWriter w;
    w.copyN(n, tmp);
  }
  ```

